the controls and permissions

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.930
that you put in
place are actually

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.139
giving you the controls you
think they're giving you?

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:05.721
And then we're going
to talk about how

00:03:05.721 --> 00:03:07.520
we wrap up some
services into solutions

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.061
and solve some common problems
that my customers have brought

00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:12.300
to me.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.450
So controls-- the first thing
we have to talk about is IAM.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.830
Identity and Access
Management is

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.470
fundamental to everything
that happens on Google Cloud

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.900
Platform and in any cloud.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.450
And the thing that took a
while for some of my customers

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.750
to tweak is that it's
very centralized.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.960
If you look at GCP, everything
is integrated with IAM.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.660
You can assign a role to
a user, and suddenly, they

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.210
might have access to
a ton of resources

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:39.541
they didn't have before.

00:03:39.541 --> 00:03:41.540
Whereas, in traditional
on-premise environments,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.873
they found that actually it
might be four or five or six

00:03:43.873 --> 00:03:44.880
different teams.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.880
The team that manages the
firewall rules opening

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.860
a port, the team that
manages the service

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.550
controls to allow
you in, the team that

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.008
then grants you permissions to
[INAUDIBLE] Active Directory

00:03:53.008 --> 00:03:55.281
access to it-- it's a
whole bunch of people.

00:03:55.281 --> 00:03:57.530
And actually, it's a bit
higher risk having everything

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.650
controlled through IAM because
you can more easily give people

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.110
the wrong access.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.860
So you really need to
put a lot more focus

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.410
into doing it right by making
sure that you're locking it

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:10.730
down a lot more than you
would necessarily on premise.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.820
So what we're
going to look at is

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.910
who can access what resources,
which sounds pretty simple.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.410
But there are lots
of questions that

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:20.389
come out of this, such as how
do you figure out what resources

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:21.430
they should be accessing?

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.060
And actually, who can
access those resources?

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.732
It's a pretty hard question
to answer most of the time.

00:04:28.732 --> 00:04:30.190
The very first
piece of advice I'll

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.490
give you is all of your access
should be based around groups.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:37.880
If a user is directly given
permission to resources,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.480
then you have a
significant burden

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.600
in monitoring and
management and trying

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.204
to understand who
can access what.

00:04:44.204 --> 00:04:46.370
I would actually say you
should be actively scanning

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.540
for and looking for any
usage of identity controls

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.450
that rely on the individual
accessing any resource.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.890
Everything through
a group allows

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:56.900
you to put in
improper JML processes

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:01.196
and correct monitoring, and it's
much easier to manage as well.

00:05:01.196 --> 00:05:02.570
And I see this
mistake being made

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.940
at the start of implementations
when people are starting

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.080
to work with Google Cloud.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.330
They go, oh, we'll just have
this one person accessing

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.070
these rules, and
these couple of people

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:11.870
can have this product over here.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.050
It's best to start strong and
stick with it consistently

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.810
as you scale and grow
your use for the platform.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:19.600
Once you're 6 or 12
months down the road,

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.490
it can be hard to back out.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.240
So just a really simple
example of a group

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.890
that some of my
customers might use

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.270
would be Global Sec Ops admins.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.950
[? There ?] are then
two roles below it.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.435
There are security admins,
and there are log viewers.

00:05:31.435 --> 00:05:32.810
You can basically
figure out what

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.020
they do based on the name of the
group, so it's self-describing.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.450
So if you are reviewing
an alert or a log that

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.310
contains the group
name, you can kind of

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:45.090
figure out what access they
have based on that group name.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.030
And secondly, understanding
folder structures

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.950
and how they fit into
the resource hierarchy

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.840
is pretty important.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.890
So just to go over this
again, if people aren't aware,

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.800
at the very top you
have your organization.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.560
There's one organization,
and that's your company.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.170
Below it, you have folders,
many folders and hierarchies.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:08.750
Below that, you have projects,
and then those projects

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.500
have the resources
inside of them.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.320
The best way to map
it up, in my opinion,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.820
and I found in most
of my customers,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.800
is to map your folder hierarchy
to your company layout

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.226
and structure.

00:06:20.226 --> 00:06:21.600
And by that, I
mean separating it

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.450
into different countries,
different working

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.840
groups, different teams, and
doing the logical separation,

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.450
and using folders
as many times as you

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.120
need to to have that
clean separation.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.460
Because when you apply
constraints and policies

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.465
at an organization-level,
and they trickle down,

00:06:37.465 --> 00:06:39.840
you can then apply it to a
folder further down the chain,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.730
and that will
trickle down as well.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.500
Another resultant
thing that I've found

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.950
is that the folder hierarchy
isn't always apparent.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.890
So for example, if you're
looking at an access control

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.440
list, if you're
looking a log, it

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.690
will come with a project name.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:57.700
But it's not always
obvious to many people,

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.750
where does that project sit
in the folder hierarchy?

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.580
So simply, just expand it
out into the project name.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.496
Structure it as
organization name.

00:07:05.496 --> 00:07:07.870
I recommend you always prefix
with your organization name

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.780
at the start, and then
expand out the folder tree

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:11.859
in the project name.

00:07:11.859 --> 00:07:13.150
Make use of long project names.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.890
It's absolutely fine.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:18.799
Simple example-- Acme Corp
have a sales organization.

00:07:18.799 --> 00:07:20.590
There's an application
running inside of it

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.180
that provides insight around
what their clients are doing.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.010
And this is the production
version of that application.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.110
It's self-describing,
it's easy to understand,

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.540
and it's easy to debug where
it sits in the folder tree

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.581
without having to go to
someone that can actually

00:07:33.581 --> 00:07:35.240
see it and give you
that information.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.614
You're trying to lower the
burden and the admin overhead.

00:07:40.112 --> 00:07:42.320
So Service Accounts is
something that I didn't really

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.250
know that much about.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.310
And I assumed I kind of knew
how they worked until I started

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:47.620
writing this presentation.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.228
And I had it completely wrong.

00:07:49.228 --> 00:07:52.760
[LAUGHS] And the real
key advice that I

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.980
had from one of the
security PMs was,

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.860
you need to think
of Service Accounts

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.527
as both a resource
and an identity.

00:08:00.527 --> 00:08:02.193
There are two
perspectives, and you need

00:08:02.193 --> 00:08:04.340
to be really aware of them.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:05.960
So when we think
about a resource,

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.750
a resource has controls as to
who can access that resource.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:13.040
So in this case, let's say
you created a Service Account.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.530
You want to limit who can
use that Service Account.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.930
So this simple diagram shows
you there's a user called Alice,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.040
and she'd like to start
a virtual machine.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.950
She'd like to start that
using a particular Service

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.930
Account that's being generated.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:31.130
So she needs a role that allows
her Service Account user to use

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.900
that Service Account.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.659
So she has a constraint on
being able to access it.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:38.000
Normally, that list of people
with Service Account user

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.909
should be pretty small
and pretty focused,

00:08:40.909 --> 00:08:43.629
and you should be reviewing
who has that access.

00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:45.170
Because as soon as
you actually start

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.960
using the Service Account,
and you start up that instance

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.820
using the Service
Account, it flips over,

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.080
and it becomes an identity.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:57.840
Then the identity has access
to resources on the other side.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.310
So that's, then, having
permissions and roles as to

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.770
can it access a Google
Cloud Storage Bucket,

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.750
can it access a
spanner database,

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:08.110
can it create some
credentials elsewhere.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:11.280
So what this means is that a
user who has Service Account

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.770
user permissions
can then actually

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.620
access all of the resources that
Service Account can access as

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.159
well, not necessarily directly.

00:09:19.159 --> 00:09:20.950
But actually, through
that Service Account,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.550
that can access everything else.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:25.260
And that can really greatly
increase the scope of access

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.700
that individual user has
through that one role.

00:09:30.522 --> 00:09:32.730
So simple tips, things that
have caught me out before

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.980
and caught out my customers--
have a naming convention.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:38.490
And one of the things I'd
say for a naming convention

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:42.180
is actually saying it's a
Service Account in the name.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:44.730
If you look at a log, or
report, or an [? ACL, ?]

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.280
it just looks like
an email address.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.830
Is it a person, or is
it a Service Account?

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.965
Just put in front
SVC, or SA, or Service

00:09:52.965 --> 00:09:56.910
to obviously delineate
this is a Service Account.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.082
The display name
is a longer name.

00:09:59.082 --> 00:10:00.540
A number of my
customers have found

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.040
putting the
particular roles given

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.230
for that Service Account
into the display name

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.270
also makes it easier to
figure out what it does

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.160
and what it should
access and, actually,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.010
how much permission does it
have to access other resources.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.752
Being verbose and
repeating yourself

00:10:12.752 --> 00:10:13.710
isn't really a problem.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.100
So then the question
becomes how many

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.660
Service Accounts do we need?

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.540
And the answer is, it
depends, which is the worst

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.820
answer you can give to anyone.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.070
So generally, as a rule of
thumb, what I am looking at

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.910
is how many rolls are given to
a particular Service Account?

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:34.420
We generally say that each of
your applications or services

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.220
should have a Service Account.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.520
If you're, then,
giving it many roles--

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.726
5, 10, 15, or 20--

00:10:40.726 --> 00:10:42.100
that's probably
a hint you should

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.720
be refactoring the application
if you can control it

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.181
or trying to decompose
it into multiple items.

00:10:48.181 --> 00:10:49.680
So what you have
to think about here

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.750
is, if someone picked
up that Service Account,

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:54.510
and they took it away,
and they used it later on,

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.180
it can be hard to monitor how
a Service Account is being used

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.096
and if there's multiple
Service Accounts being

00:10:59.096 --> 00:11:00.530
used in multiple places.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.145
And if that one account can
access 10 different resources,

00:11:04.145 --> 00:11:06.270
then that's a concentration
risk that you should be

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:07.670
looking at a lot more closely.

00:11:10.352 --> 00:11:13.209
And don't rely the
default Service Accounts.

00:11:13.209 --> 00:11:14.750
So whenever you
create a new project,

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:16.980
and you spin up a
Compute Engine API,

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:18.660
it will create you
a default Service

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:22.170
Account that's really useful
for testing and development.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.660
We do not guarantee
the functionality

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.932
of that Service Account, the
roles and scopes it might have.

00:11:28.932 --> 00:11:31.140
We also don't guarantee it
might exist in the future,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.900
whether we create that default
Service Account or not.

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.780
If anything today relies on
the default Service Account,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.480
you should immediately stop
using it and transition

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.390
to custom Service Accounts that
you can control because there

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:43.570
may be a breaking change.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.550
So something I actually
learned on Monday this week--

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:50.590
and added this
slide on Tuesday--

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.210
was trying to
answer the question

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.970
of who can access what?

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:58.960
So which user can access
this particular resource?

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:01.660
Or who could delete
this virtual machine

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.132
is a really hard
question to answer.

00:12:04.132 --> 00:12:07.190
And there's a great piece
of software called Forseti.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.730
Forseti's open-source
software originally

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:14.510
developed by Google Cloud that
we use to monitor our policies.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:17.042
And we need to answer
those questions ourselves.

00:12:17.042 --> 00:12:18.500
It's pretty simple
in how it works.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.210
We do an inventory.

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:21.770
We take a list of
all the users, we

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.190
take a list of
all the groups, we

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.910
take a list of all
the roles, we take

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.570
a list of all of the resources.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.940
We build a model on
top of it, and then you

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:30.320
can query that model.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.450
And you can directly
ask it, who could

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.490
delete this virtual machine?

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.680
For this user, what
can they access?

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.250
Who has Service Account
user permissions?

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.710
It's a really quick way to get
answers to those questions.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.080
It's freely available
open-source software online.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.912
I'd highly recommend
you review it.

00:12:48.912 --> 00:12:51.090
So that was control,
and that was really

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:53.290
centered around identity
and access management.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.011
And as you'll see as
we go through this,

00:12:55.011 --> 00:12:56.760
everything builds on
top of it when you're

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.910
looking at cloud security.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.810
So for visibility, we're going
to talk a lot around logging

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:06.570
trying to understand
which users are actually

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.760
using those permissions,
and who is doing what

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.380
in the operational perspective?

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.476
The main tool here
is Stackdriver.

00:13:14.476 --> 00:13:16.100
Stackdriver has a
ton of functionality.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.060
It's a fantastic platform that
does a million things really

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:19.561
well.

00:13:19.561 --> 00:13:21.310
There are some great
announcements at Next

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:22.407
this week as what it does.

00:13:22.407 --> 00:13:24.490
But we're going to talk
about a subsection of it--

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.680
Stackdriver Logging.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.120
So Stackdriver
Monitoring is generally

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:30.940
for the operations teams.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:33.260
It's asking you
how much CPU usage

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.180
is on this particular machine?

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:38.510
How much latency is on this
particular load balancer?

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:41.760
Stackdriver Logging takes,
generally, human-readable logs

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.700
in text form or JSON
format, parses them,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.350
and makes them
available for developers

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:49.680
debugging an application and,
typically, security teams who

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.710
want to look at what's
changed, how did it change,

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:55.905
and how does that affect me?

00:13:55.905 --> 00:13:59.060
So on GCP, there are a number
of different logging platforms

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.216
and a number of different
logs that are produced.

00:14:02.216 --> 00:14:04.090
The two we're going to
really talk about here

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:08.740
are Admin Activity Logs
and Data Access Logs.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:12.010
Admin Activity Logs
are on by default,

00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:14.490
and you can't turn them off.

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:17.880
So whenever you make a change
through the Admin Console,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:19.920
whenever you run
a GCloud command,

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.140
whenever you do something
that changes your platform

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.250
in any way, that's logged for
you, and you can't stop that.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:27.980
That's a good thing,
and it's free.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.450
It's baked into the cost.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:33.600
What's also available
is Data Access Logging.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.990
So Data Access Logging looks on
top of the platform and says,

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:41.070
well, inside your applications,
we can log what happens.

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.650
An example, Cloud SQL--

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.980
if I go and create a Cloud SQL
cluster and create some nodes,

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:49.710
that will be logged through
the Admin Activity Logs.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:53.010
If I actually send a query
to my Cloud SQL database,

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:55.590
that can be logged inside
the Data Access Logs.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.780
We can log if it's
a READ query, we

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:01.210
can log if it's a change, if
it's a modify or a delete.

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:04.120
And this is fairly consistent
across most of our products.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.720
But it's not enabled
by default, and it can

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.600
produce a huge amount of logs.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.992
You need to be really aware
of how much this can produce.

00:15:10.992 --> 00:15:12.450
And we're going to
talk in a second

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.715
about reducing that volume
to a manageable quantity.

00:15:17.422 --> 00:15:19.880
So the first thing to say is
you need different permissions

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:21.770
to access the Data Access Logs.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.860
Because they're inherently more
sensitive because they contain

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:27.126
data access and data change.

00:15:27.126 --> 00:15:28.500
So the default
logging permission

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.890
you're going to give to your
ops teams and developers

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:32.210
is logging.viewer.

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:34.400
They can view the logs.

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:36.290
To get Data Access
Logging access,

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:39.340
you need
logging.privatelogviewer.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:40.670
It's in the name.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:42.170
It's a lot more
private information.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.840
It should be a much
smaller subset of people

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.690
because they're looking at
actually this data access

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:51.610
and who's changing
the data access.

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.640
So a quick example of
how you can turn this on

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:54.848
and how you can configure it.

00:15:54.848 --> 00:15:58.380
You can do it through a GUI, or
you can do it through GCloud.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.990
And you can configure this
at an organizational level

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.510
and speed it down the
hierarchy, or you can do it

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:05.440
on a per-project basis.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.070
So in this example, we're doing
it for one particular project.

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:12.240
So I'm pulling out the IAM
policy for myproject123.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.790
I didn't follow my own
naming convention advice.

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:17.700
We feed it into a yaml
file, and then we modify it,

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:20.245
and we just append at the
bottom the audit configuration.

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:23.700
And all we're saying is we're
going have, for every service,

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:28.540
enable DATA_READ, DATA_WRITE,
and also ADMIN_READ.

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:31.170
So ADMIN_READ is every
time you run a READ command

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:32.650
through GCloud.

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.370
So if you list all of the
virtual machine instances

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:37.770
you have, that's an ADMIN_READ.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.990
If you create a
virtual machine, that's

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.810
an admin change
and an ADMIN_WRITE.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.845
So simply, we change the yaml
file, we write it back in,

00:16:45.845 --> 00:16:47.220
and that project
will now produce

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.960
logs for all the DATA_READ
and all the DATA_WRITE

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:51.510
for every single
service you have.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:54.347
And that is a huge
amount of information.

00:16:54.347 --> 00:16:55.930
Could be good, could
be bad, depending

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:57.720
on what you want to monitor.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.780
Typically, you want to
exclude certain services.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:02.880
So building on
this example, we're

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:06.000
going to show you how you can
pull out, say, for Cloud SQL,

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.280
how do we remove one
particular Service Account

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:11.360
from logging those
Data Access Logs?

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:13.609
So the use case here is you
have an application that's

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:15.560
serving an API.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:17.060
And you've written it in Python.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.800
It's serving your users.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:21.579
Behind the scenes is
a Cloud SQL database.

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:24.740
And that Cloud SQL database is
the primary storage system--

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:27.680
lots of reads, lots of
writes coming through.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.870
Your application is
running under the Service

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.124
Account you created, your
custom Service Account.

00:17:32.124 --> 00:17:34.040
Actually, this is the
default Service Account.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:35.340
I should have changed that.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.090
And what you want to
say is, let's exempt

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:41.540
this particular Service Account
for Cloud SQL from DATA_READ

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:43.070
and DATA_WRITE.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:46.790
So what you're left
with is any data access

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:48.560
not by the production
Service Account

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.229
running your application.

00:17:50.229 --> 00:17:51.645
So it means that
you'll get access

00:17:51.645 --> 00:17:55.640
and logs every time a human
or a different service

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.880
tries to access or change
data in that database.

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:01.320
So instantly, that's kind of
interesting, saying, well,

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:04.280
my DBA has now changed something
in a production database,

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:07.340
or an engineer is logged in and
runs some queries against it.

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:10.100
That isn't the typical
flow that we'd expect.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:13.450
That's worth monitoring.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:15.120
You can also do this
through the GUI--

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.740
so through the IAM
console, you can go in,

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:19.510
you can turn on the
default audit logs,

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.700
you can turn on the
DATA_READ and WRITE logs,

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.210
you can choose
particular services,

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:25.490
you can build the exemptions.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.950
I'd recommend this for
testing to figure out

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:29.644
how many logs does it produce.

00:18:29.644 --> 00:18:31.310
It's a good way to
figure out, actually,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:33.500
is it a couple of megs,
or a couple of gigs,

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.110
or a couple of terabytes
we're dealing with here?

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:38.480
But normally, you want to do
this programmatically most

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.730
of the time, particularly,
dealing with exceptions.

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.260
When you're looking at
Data Access Logging,

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.176
you should be doing it
on a case-by-case basis

00:18:44.176 --> 00:18:46.640
most of the time.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.160
ADMIN_READ logs could be
turned on across the platform.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:54.072
Again, be aware of how
much volume it can produce.

00:18:54.072 --> 00:18:56.030
So another challenge my
customers brought to me

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:59.780
was, by default, logs in
GCP live within the project

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:01.460
they're created in.

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:03.410
If you're an enterprise,
you could well

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:06.350
have hundreds or thousands
of different projects created

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:08.070
in many different countries.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:09.860
And that becomes
an admin overhead

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:11.480
having to go to
every single project

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.370
to look at the
logs inside of it.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.140
And it stops you
correlating them together

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:16.640
and looking for
interesting patterns

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:18.870
across your environment.

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:22.130
So with Stackdriver, we can
do aggregated log exporting.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.680
So simply, we can take the logs
from lots of different member

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.660
projects, put them
through some filters,

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:31.350
so the filters could be to only
forward on particular services,

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:34.010
particular users,
particular accounts.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:35.080
You could sample it down.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:37.130
So you could say,
just send 10% of it.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:38.890
That's only interesting to me.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:41.360
And there are three different
sources you can send it to--

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.250
sorry, destinations.

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.320
There's cloud storage,
so you can write it just

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.150
as plain text files.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.980
Commonly, this is
done for compliance.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:52.280
We need to store this for three
years, five years, seven years,

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.170
as cheap as possible
so it's available

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:57.310
if we did need to do an
investigation down the line.

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:59.200
You can send it to
BigQuery so that you

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.477
can run SQL queries against
it and do some analysis.

00:20:01.477 --> 00:20:02.560
And this is pretty common.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.930
I'll talk to you about an
example of that in a minute.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:06.970
You can also send it to Pub/Sub.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.550
And Pub/Sub is typically
where third-party platforms

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:10.610
integrate.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:12.409
So tools like Splunk
have connectors

00:20:12.409 --> 00:20:14.950
that read from Pub/Sub to take
those logs out the other side.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.590
So let's assume that
all of your projects

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:22.830
are sending their logs to
one particular Cloud Storage

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:23.470
bucket.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:25.740
You want to do your
archiving compliance

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.974
and make them stay
there for seven years.

00:20:27.974 --> 00:20:29.640
There's a couple of
things I'd recommend

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:33.210
to make sure those logs are
sound and safe and secure.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:35.990
The first thing is turning on
Object Versioning on the Google

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:38.400
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.230
The reason for
that is that, when

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:42.510
you turn on Object
Versioning, you can't actually

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.250
delete files anymore.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:47.122
You can run the delete command,
but that file's never deleted.

00:20:47.122 --> 00:20:48.580
The reference to
it says it's gone,

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:50.460
but the file is still there.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.440
If someone accidentally
changed the file,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:53.940
we keep all of the
previous versions

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:56.190
as well, so you never
actually lose anything

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:57.784
as long as that's turned on.

00:20:57.784 --> 00:20:59.200
And of course,
this bucket, you'll

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:00.610
lock down permissions on it.

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:02.068
The project it's
running in, you'll

00:21:02.068 --> 00:21:04.230
lock down the access to
this project as well.

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:05.604
And there's another
level you can

00:21:05.604 --> 00:21:09.336
apply where we can put
controls on the project itself.

00:21:09.336 --> 00:21:10.710
So you can apply
something called

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:15.150
a Lien, which essentially says
you can't delete this project.

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.460
It's really simple to
apply, and it's just

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:21.030
another level of control
against accidental deletion

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:23.412
or potentially
malicious deletion.

00:21:23.412 --> 00:21:24.620
It's a really simple command.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:26.760
It's done by Resource Manager.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:28.460
You do it on a
per-project basis.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.130
And I'd highly recommend doing
this for all of your production

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:33.470
projects, for all of your
applications that are running.

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:35.990
Anything that just doesn't
need to be deleted,

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:39.380
accidentally, I'd recommend
turning this flag on.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:41.600
All that happens is, if
you run a command to delete

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:43.760
the project, it comes
back with an error saying,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.250
no, that can't be done.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.000
And you can give
a specific reason

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.804
as to why that can't be done.

00:21:48.804 --> 00:21:50.720
I probably wouldn't call
it Super Secret Logs,

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.709
but I might call it something a
bit more generic just in case.

00:21:53.709 --> 00:21:56.000
But that will at least stop
you from both malicious and

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.400
accidental deletions, trying
to make sure your logs are

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:01.220
safe and sound.

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:02.390
Cool.

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:05.350
So I want to talk about
security solutions.

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:07.232
And by solutions,
I mean combining

00:22:07.232 --> 00:22:08.690
lots of different
elements together

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:13.550
to solve common challenges
that my customers had.

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:15.590
The first and
probably most common

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:18.440
conversation I have
with people is, how do I

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:20.930
run my applications securely?

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:24.260
So I have a line, a business
application, it's HR

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:27.440
application or
finance application,

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:29.120
and it's out there,
and our users

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:32.809
should be able to access this
from the corporate environment.

00:22:32.809 --> 00:22:34.350
Really, where we
want to get them to,

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:35.886
is the Beyondcorp model.

00:22:35.886 --> 00:22:37.510
If you're not familiar
with Beyondcorp,

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.180
I highly recommend doing
some research into it

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:42.920
or attending the session that
we have running at the moment

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:44.490
or watching the replay.

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:47.920
Beyondcorp is talking about
beyond the corporate network.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:50.170
How can we have all of our
projects and applications

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:53.200
running in a way that people
can access it from anywhere

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.840
without being behind a
particular IP address,

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:58.120
without having to dial
up VPN into the office

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.710
and access it from
trusted IP ranges, which

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:03.230
doesn't work at scale?

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.170
So when we start to take
people on the journey

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:08.080
towards a
Beyondcorp-style model,

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:10.460
the first thing that
comes up is VPNs.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:11.624
People don't like VPNs.

00:23:11.624 --> 00:23:13.540
And it's having the
constraints around people,

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:16.150
either remotely
accessing in, or dealing

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:19.090
with just the admin
overhead of managing it.

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.070
How can we start to
build applications

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.197
that take advantage
of Google's scale

00:23:23.197 --> 00:23:25.780
so you can take advantage of our
denial-of-service protection,

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:28.240
so you can deal with
our scalable networking

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:31.320
without having a
bottleneck in place?

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:33.740
So there are three components
we're going to put together.

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:35.590
The first one is Cloud
Armor, and that's

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:37.030
protecting the edge--

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:40.080
so limiting who and
how they can access.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:42.600
We're going to talk about
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:45.880
so we can make sure that only
users that are authenticated

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:47.940
and authorized to
access the application

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:49.854
can access the application.

00:23:49.854 --> 00:23:52.020
And then we're going to
talk about VPC firewall-- so

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:54.395
how do you actually restrict
the ports and protocols that

00:23:54.395 --> 00:23:57.660
can communicate with the
application you're running?

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:59.190
So in a really
simplified example,

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:01.169
it looks a little bit like this.

00:24:01.169 --> 00:24:02.960
So this is kind of step
one of the journey.

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:05.501
So let's imagine you have your
employee in your San Francisco

00:24:05.501 --> 00:24:06.270
office.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.400
And the external IP
address from that office

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:12.860
is 1.2.3.4 to make
life easy for everyone.

00:24:12.860 --> 00:24:15.644
The first thing that would
happen with that traffic is,

00:24:15.644 --> 00:24:17.060
hopefully, it will
get routed over

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:19.380
to the Google Cloud Platform.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:24.010
The first place it meets is
our Edge proxies or edge PoPs.

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:28.024
And Cloud Armor sits on top
of them as a bolt on service.

00:24:28.024 --> 00:24:29.690
So everything we're
talking about here--

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:32.200
so Identity-Aware
Proxy and Cloud Armor--

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:34.679
relies on, not changing
your architecture.

00:24:34.679 --> 00:24:36.220
We're not telling
you to install a VM

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:37.510
and push your
traffic through it.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.910
We're trying to layer the
capabilities onto your existing

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:42.615
design and the existing
capabilities we have today.

00:24:42.615 --> 00:24:44.740
We don't believe you should
change your application

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.400
to get more security.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:47.900
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:50.030
is we're just going to do simple
whitelisting and blacklisting

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:50.919
through Cloud Armor.

00:24:50.919 --> 00:24:52.460
We're just going to
allow connections

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:53.854
from particular IP addresses.

00:24:53.854 --> 00:24:56.270
In this case, we'll just do
it for your corporate ranges--

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:59.980
so 1.2.3.4 can connect through.

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:02.770
And that will allow it to
initiate a TLS session using

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:05.750
Google networking
technologies to a HTTPS load

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:08.830
balancer serving your
particular application

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:11.310
and your particular project.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.930
So then we're going to add
on Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:16.620
Identity-Aware Proxy
is actually the service

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.287
that we released that's
as close to Beyondcorp

00:25:19.287 --> 00:25:21.120
as we have internally
at Google as possible.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:23.580
And as a great roadmap
for Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:25.830
it's really coming
on leaps and bounds.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:27.890
The concept is this.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:30.860
You should provide network-based
authentication and network

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:34.600
restriction based on the
identity of the user.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:37.530
So if I'm an employee,
and I work in HR,

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:39.540
the ideal scenario
is the only network

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:41.970
services I can ever
talk to are those

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:45.240
which relate to my job
and my function in HR.

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:47.825
There might be tens or hundreds
or thousands of applications

00:25:47.825 --> 00:25:49.300
that are running out there.

00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:52.080
And normally, you can ping
them, you can talk to them.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:55.540
You probably can't log into
it, but it's a very wide net

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:57.330
that you could touch.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:00.950
Identity-Aware Proxy looks
at your user credentials,

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:02.360
looks at the roles
and the groups

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.345
that you're associated with.

00:26:04.345 --> 00:26:05.720
You can integrate
it because it's

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:08.460
backed off by Cloud Identity.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.450
So you can use Active
Directory Federated Services.

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:13.190
You can use two-factor
authentication

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:14.439
and single sign-on.

00:26:14.439 --> 00:26:15.980
And then it says,
well, actually, you

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:17.600
can't get through
this load balancer,

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:20.330
you can't get through
this proxy unless your job

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:23.392
role and function
and group is correct.

00:26:23.392 --> 00:26:25.100
So it means, actually,
the attack surface

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:28.130
is significantly reduced
because no longer does

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.560
any employee have network
connectivity to all

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:32.300
of the applications
that are out there.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:35.720
You're no longer reliant on
the application authenticating

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:38.120
and authorizing those services.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:39.590
Because as we all
know, is there's

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:41.930
a particular vulnerability
in some part of the stack,

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:43.940
in some middleware
messaging platform,

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:46.580
that any of those desktops,
if they were compromised,

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:48.429
are going to sweep
the network, try

00:26:48.429 --> 00:26:50.720
and find one particular
application service environment

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:52.470
that has that
vulnerability, and then they

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:53.810
have a way into your network.

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:55.730
With this, you've
suddenly restricted

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:58.580
that down to a small subset
of your application servers

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:00.440
that are only
hosting the services

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:03.050
they should be able to access.

00:27:03.050 --> 00:27:05.390
So it's issuing the pass-through
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:07.690
and they are allowed
to access the service.

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:10.760
Next up, again, we're
going to use Cloud Armor.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:12.860
So what Cloud Armor
can do is that, now we

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:14.780
know that this is
a trusted user,

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:16.850
and it's a HTTPS
load balancer, we

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.660
can inspect the
contents of the packet.

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:23.010
So we can do some simple
SQL injection and checking.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.649
We can look for
cross-site scripting.

00:27:24.649 --> 00:27:26.190
We can just check
the packets, seeing

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:28.870
that they're the right
size, and shape, and volume,

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:31.560
and sort of conform
to standard bounds.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:33.360
If that looks good,
we'll then pass it

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:35.700
through to the service
on the back end.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:37.380
In this instance, we
use Compute Engine

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.640
so it's running a simple
infrastructure as a service.

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:41.810
Could be Kubernetes
Engine, could be App

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:46.410
Engine Flexible, which, in turn,
will have the VPC firewalls.

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:48.325
And those VPC
firewalls will allow

00:27:48.325 --> 00:27:51.030
you to restrict ports and
protocols at a networking

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:53.600
level.

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.030
So the next question I
normally get from people is--

00:27:56.030 --> 00:27:56.530
OK.

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:59.120
So let's assume we're trying to
move away from IP whitelisting

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.230
and blacklisting.

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.382
We want to promote
employee mobility

00:28:02.382 --> 00:28:03.590
so people can work from home.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:06.380
We're not having to get them
to dial up to our remote office

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:08.340
range and connect through there.

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.810
But we only want people
to access it from the US,

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:14.420
or if they're in the
UK, only in the UK.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:16.670
So again, Cloud Armor
has functionality.

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:18.461
You can write a
simple rule that says

00:28:18.461 --> 00:28:20.960
we're only going to let you in
if the origin of your traffic

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:24.119
comes from this or these
particular countries.

00:28:24.119 --> 00:28:26.660
Or on the flip side, we don't
like these particular countries

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:28.750
for you to come from.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.724
So this is kind of step
two on the journey.

00:28:30.724 --> 00:28:32.890
You're removing the hard
IP whitelisting and saying,

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:34.840
right, well, actually,
now my employees--

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:36.340
I know they're
working in the United

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:38.340
States, which is better for me.

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:40.674
They're going through, and
we're validating the packets.

00:28:40.674 --> 00:28:42.756
We know that they're doing
the right job function.

00:28:42.756 --> 00:28:44.850
We know they're using
two-factor authentication.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:47.433
And finally, they can actually
access the application services

00:28:47.433 --> 00:28:48.349
we're running.

00:28:48.349 --> 00:28:50.390
I generally find, talking
to my customers, that's

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:52.100
a high level of security
than they'd ever

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:54.590
been able to build
with some kind of DMZ

00:28:54.590 --> 00:28:56.819
they've had for
the last 15 years.

00:28:56.819 --> 00:28:58.610
And this is all scalable
Google technology.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.950
You're not relying on a
single point of failure.

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:07.080
You're not reliant on managing
traditional technologies.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:09.980
Another thing you can do now
is you can enforce standards

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:12.380
around SSL and TLS.

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:15.560
So attached to the HTTPS load
balancer that we're running,

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:18.710
you can simply state the
ciphers that you're allowed

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:20.500
and the versions of
TLS that you'll accept

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:22.560
for clients to connect to it.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:25.190
So you can enforce a
minimum of TLS 1.2.

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:27.170
You can add or remove
the particular ciphers

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:30.062
that you'd like people to use
to ensure that those clients--

00:29:30.062 --> 00:29:32.270
if they are working from
home and they're employees--

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:33.770
and hopefully, they're in the
right country and they've got

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:34.940
the right roles--

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:37.430
are using good
encryption in transit

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:40.160
that you trust and validate
and conforms to your standards.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.670
So another question that
comes from this is--

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:45.170
OK.

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:47.930
So we're running our service in
more of a remote access model,

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:51.467
we're using Identity-Aware
Proxy, we're using Cloud Armor,

00:29:51.467 --> 00:29:54.050
we're allowing people to access
without this corporate network

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:55.320
any more.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.380
So where are they
connecting from?

00:29:57.380 --> 00:29:59.030
Where does the traffic go to?

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.640
And actually, how do my services
interact with each other,

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:03.630
and what are they doing?

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:05.990
So VPC Flow Logging
is typically how

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:07.970
we get this information out.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:11.810
VPC Flow Logging, you turn
it on on a per-subnet basis

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:14.590
and record four different
types of traffic.

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:18.680
We record inter-VPC-- so any
traffic within the subnet--

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.770
intra-VPC-- traffic
between multiple subnets,

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:23.660
assuming the logging
is turned on--

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:25.850
traffic to and from
Google services--

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:28.730
so sending requests to BigQuery
and getting responses--

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:30.360
and also internet traffic--

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:33.950
so internet, egress, and ingress
between your applications

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:35.369
and services.

00:30:35.369 --> 00:30:37.410
And all of these logs
[? I've heard ?] before are

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:42.080
going to Stackdriver logging,
and they're pretty valuable.

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:45.020
So the quickest way to
get some insight into them

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:46.490
is to feed them
out into BigQuery

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:48.320
as we talked about earlier.

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:51.680
Use aggregated log exports
for each of your projects,

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:55.240
set it up to feed the logs
out, aggregate the logs,

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:58.070
set up the filters to send
just the VPC Flow Logs out

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:01.379
into BigQuery so they're
immediately available to query,

00:31:01.379 --> 00:31:03.170
and we're going to use
Data Studio to build

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:04.836
a nice little report
on top of it to try

00:31:04.836 --> 00:31:07.260
and figure out
what's in the logs.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:09.470
So here's a little dashboard
that I pulled together

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.297
with a sample application
that I spun up.

00:31:11.297 --> 00:31:13.130
I didn't put any country
restrictions on it.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:14.510
I was just interested
to see who's

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:16.500
going to start pinging it around
in a "Hello, World" application

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:17.640
that I spun up.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:20.420
So you can see here that it
was running in two regions.

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:23.210
We got Europe West
1 and Europe West 2.

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:26.004
We had connections from a
lot of different countries.

00:31:26.004 --> 00:31:27.920
We can see the round-trip
time for the packets

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:29.220
that were coming through.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.280
There's a huge amount of
detail and information

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:34.510
that's really valuable
inside these logs.

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:37.070
And typically, building this
in non-cloud environments

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:39.260
requires pretty
expensive technology,

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:40.400
fairly specialized vendors.

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:42.358
And dealing with actually
the volume of traffic

00:31:42.358 --> 00:31:45.470
it can produce, VPC Flow Logging
can produce a decent amount

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:48.530
of information, and you can mine
some really interesting trends

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:51.590
in it, particularly, when you're
looking across your projects

00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:53.720
and across your
applications, looking

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:55.715
for common types
of traffic, looking

00:31:55.715 --> 00:31:58.340
for particular applications that
are connecting to you that you

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:01.100
don't like, looking for
particular ports and protocols

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:03.560
that you're not expecting.

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:05.810
So then that leads
to another use case.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:08.250
Which services
talk to each other?

00:32:08.250 --> 00:32:10.770
So in a world where
things are very connected,

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:12.944
there are lots of
dependencies in around it.

00:32:12.944 --> 00:32:15.110
There are some great
announcements at Next this week

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:16.910
talking around Istio.

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:18.830
So Istio is fantastic
if you're running

00:32:18.830 --> 00:32:22.052
in a microservices
environment inside Kubernetes.

00:32:22.052 --> 00:32:24.010
Many of my customers are
not at that stage yet.

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:25.790
They're using traditional
virtual machines

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:27.590
and infrastructure,
and they're talking

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:29.540
to each other in all
kinds of wonderful ways,

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:32.070
and they just kind of hope
they know what they're doing.

00:32:32.070 --> 00:32:33.600
So if you're using
VPC Flow Logging,

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:35.930
we turn on the logging for
all the different subnets.

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:38.090
And now we can start to
profile the applications.

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:40.880
And we can figure out
which ports and protocols

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:42.170
are being used?

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:44.800
How much traffic is
being sent between them?

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:47.380
And one of my customers
took this even further,

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:51.170
took the aggregated exports and
the summaries of those flows,

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:53.480
and then turned them
into VPC firewall rules,

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:56.180
and applied them, and
locked it down as a policy.

00:32:56.180 --> 00:32:59.660
So they monitored it for a
period of around two weeks.

00:32:59.660 --> 00:33:01.094
They found every
single connection

00:33:01.094 --> 00:33:02.760
that they saw between
these applications

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:04.551
and then built very
specific firewall walls

00:33:04.551 --> 00:33:06.350
to limit that
connectivity to only

00:33:06.350 --> 00:33:08.910
those particular
approved connections.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:11.920
So what they found was that,
when the application changed,

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:15.050
and then they found a new
connection was coming through,

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:17.690
the question becomes,
is it a developer?

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:20.060
Can we correlate this
with an update or a patch

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:21.410
to the software we were using?

00:33:21.410 --> 00:33:22.170
OK, fine.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:24.503
We just need to update the
documentation and the release

00:33:24.503 --> 00:33:26.810
process better to
incorporate that change.

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:28.610
Or is it nefarious activity?

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:31.154
Has someone compromised our
application, and now, they're

00:33:31.154 --> 00:33:33.320
trying to move around to
see what other services are

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.540
around they can
go and connect to?

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:37.480
Answering that question
is pretty critical.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:39.063
And this gives you
a lot of visibility

00:33:39.063 --> 00:33:42.575
to see that very quickly and
then do the investigation.

00:33:46.535 --> 00:33:51.640
So a use case I saw recently
where the customer was,

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.414
how do I keep my secrets secret?

00:33:54.414 --> 00:33:56.540
And we're going to use
Cloud KMS for this.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:58.580
And it's really
important to share,

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:02.330
you cannot store secrets
inside Cloud KMS.

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:03.950
But it's an enabling
technology that

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:06.660
will help you keep
your secrets secret.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:09.010
The use case here was API keys.

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:10.600
So an application
that was being built

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:13.960
talked to a third-party service
that authenticated an API key.

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:15.876
In this case, it
was sending emails.

00:34:15.876 --> 00:34:17.500
The development in
the test environment

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:19.849
had one key that had
a limited quota on it,

00:34:19.849 --> 00:34:21.429
a limited amount of spend.

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:23.892
Production had another key.

00:34:23.892 --> 00:34:25.350
And they simply
wanted to make sure

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:27.957
that that key wasn't
distributive widely.

00:34:27.957 --> 00:34:30.540
They definitely didn't want to
keep it inside the source code,

00:34:30.540 --> 00:34:32.290
because then every
developer could access.

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:34.730
And they didn't quite
know where to put it.

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:36.909
So the recommended solution
for this-- and this

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:40.510
is on a public documentation--
is building two projects

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:41.519
to separate the duties.

00:34:41.519 --> 00:34:43.060
Now, actually, there
are three if you

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:45.690
think about the application
it's running as well.

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:47.949
So on the left-hand side,
we have the Secret Storage

00:34:47.949 --> 00:34:49.570
Project, and we're
going to store them

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:51.370
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:54.040
On the right-hand side, we
have the Cloud KMS Project,

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:56.670
which is the encryption
and decryption.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.380
And the nice thing here is
that one person or one team

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:01.240
can manage the keys.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:02.740
Another person or
another team can

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:05.050
manage the encrypted secrets.

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:08.800
So the first thing that you
do is you take your API key,

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:11.609
you send it to Cloud KMS, you
pick the particular key and key

00:35:11.609 --> 00:35:13.900
ring that you want, and it
gives you the encrypted blob

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.040
to store.

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:17.550
You then place it in Google
Cloud Storage in a bucket

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:19.510
as the encrypted blob.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:22.110
When your application starts
up, it does two things.

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:23.860
First, it connects to
Google Cloud Storage

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:26.085
and reads that blob
from the bucket.

00:35:26.085 --> 00:35:28.210
You obviously set up all
the permissions and scopes

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:30.640
and roles developed
to pull that down.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.070
If any other person had
access to that bucket

00:35:33.070 --> 00:35:35.470
and logged in and saw
it, it would be no use,

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:37.178
because they couldn't
actually see what's

00:35:37.178 --> 00:35:38.580
inside that encrypted blob.

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:40.840
So the next step is
it goes to Cloud KMS,

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:42.815
it sends the encrypted
blob, and then

00:35:42.815 --> 00:35:44.650
it returns a decrypted
version of it,

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:47.290
and says here's your API key.

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:49.030
So what you've done
is you delinked it.

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:51.280
You've brought in a
separation of the duties.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:53.350
Someone can independently
rotate the keys

00:35:53.350 --> 00:35:55.150
and manage the keys for you.

00:35:55.150 --> 00:35:58.000
Another team can manage the
encrypted blobs and secrets,

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:00.340
then the application can
run fairly statelessly.

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:02.550
It doesn't need to be
bundled with the secrets.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:04.150
Every time it
starts and restarts,

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:06.140
it goes and grabs
that key again.

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:07.600
And of course,
this is all logged.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:10.300
So you can start to look
for activity and say, well,

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.420
was there any failed
decryption attempts?

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:15.649
Was anyone pulling down
these particular keys

00:36:15.649 --> 00:36:17.940
from a different application
in a different source that

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:19.606
wasn't, then, going
and decrypting them?

00:36:19.606 --> 00:36:23.460
You can get some pretty
good visibility there.

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:26.020
So the final solution I'm
going to talked about,

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:28.210
which is a really
common question is,

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:30.077
are my machines up to date?

00:36:30.077 --> 00:36:31.910
And we're talking about
infrastructure here,

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:34.910
so we're talking about
plain old virtual machines.

00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:36.830
And the assumption is
that they need updating,

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:38.660
they need patching,
and also, they

00:36:38.660 --> 00:36:41.100
need to conform to your
security standards.

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:43.400
So there's the baseline
that you define.

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:46.900
The baseline might be a
NIST or a CIS standard,

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.070
and probably your
organization-specific

00:36:49.070 --> 00:36:50.516
requirements as well.

00:36:50.516 --> 00:36:53.140
But let's say there's been some
particular vulnerability occur,

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.860
and you want to
update GLIBC or libssl

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:57.764
to make sure it's maintained.

00:36:57.764 --> 00:36:59.180
So there are two
questions really.

00:36:59.180 --> 00:37:01.759
How do to make sure people
are only using trusted images?

00:37:01.759 --> 00:37:03.800
And secondly, how do you
make sure they're always

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:06.620
using the latest version?

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:08.120
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:10.790
is we're going to
build custom images.

00:37:10.790 --> 00:37:13.667
So part of Compute Engine
includes an image registry.

00:37:13.667 --> 00:37:15.500
It's not actually call
it an image registry,

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:18.770
but it is an image registry
inside of Compute Engine.

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:21.710
And with this, you can
store baked images.

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:23.450
Typically, it's an
automated process

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:25.790
that will go through and
pull down the patches,

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:28.041
build them together, do
your security configurations

00:37:28.041 --> 00:37:29.290
and hardening, and publish it.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:31.460
It could also be a
manual process as well,

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:32.967
depending on volume.

00:37:32.967 --> 00:37:34.550
And you can choose
whatever flavor you

00:37:34.550 --> 00:37:37.160
like-- particular versions
of Linux, or Windows,

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:39.740
or whatever takes your fancy--
and publish those custom

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:41.810
images, share
them, and make them

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:45.620
available for other users and
other projects to start with.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:47.560
So in this case, a
user that has access

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:49.840
to start a virtual
machine, an instance,

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:53.320
pulls the image from the image
registry based on the URL

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:57.060
and then builds it
from that version.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:38:00.180
There's a constraint you can
apply in Resource Manager that

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:03.630
allows you to control which
particular versions and which

00:38:03.630 --> 00:38:05.950
particular images can we use?

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.280
And this is important
because, by default, Google

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:10.740
published a whole range
of images for you.

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.860
We've produce standard
Ubuntu, standard CentOS,

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:15.875
standard Windows server.

00:38:15.875 --> 00:38:18.000
And that doesn't conform
to your security standards

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:19.094
and policies.

00:38:19.094 --> 00:38:21.510
It's patched, and it's updated,
and it's fairly up to date

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:22.767
with all the vulnerabilities.

00:38:22.767 --> 00:38:25.350
But it probably doesn't contain
your particular configurations

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:27.910
and your endpoint tools
and management tools.

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:29.910
So you can apply this
constraint and essentially

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:32.550
say, none of my
users can use any

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:35.330
of the Google-provided
libraries.

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:37.620
They can use none of the
Google-provided images.

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:39.330
It's completely denied.

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:41.220
So what they're left
with is they can only

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:45.240
access the custom versions
which you've published.

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:48.390
So then, what you're saying is
that any developer, any person

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:50.310
anywhere in your
organization that

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:53.760
wants to spin up an instance
has to use an instance that

00:38:53.760 --> 00:38:56.190
comes from our custom
images which we've approved,

00:38:56.190 --> 00:38:59.940
and we've standardized,
and we've pushed out.

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:01.470
So images go
through a lifecycle.

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:04.050
There are four different
states that they go through.

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:05.670
By default, they're active.

00:39:05.670 --> 00:39:08.200
You create a new image,
people can use it--

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:09.750
happy days.

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:11.760
After that, you
can deprecate it.

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:14.190
And you can say that
it's allowed to be used,

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:17.670
and often, people will support
two or three old versions,

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:20.220
but they'll get a warning saying
that this is not the latest

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:21.342
version.

00:39:21.342 --> 00:39:22.800
And typically,
people access images

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:24.300
based on the name or the family.

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:26.340
They'll say just give me
the latest version of Ubuntu

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:28.650
rather than the particular
version that's being produced.

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:30.066
But this will tell
them, actually,

00:39:30.066 --> 00:39:32.510
the version you just specified,
if they've hard-coded it

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:35.550
in a version, that's not OK.

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:37.701
Obsolete will actually
stop them using it.

00:39:37.701 --> 00:39:39.450
So if they try and
start an instance using

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.134
that particular image that's
now obsolete, it will fail,

00:39:42.134 --> 00:39:43.800
it won't start up,
and it will give them

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:45.780
a failure instead of a warning.

00:39:45.780 --> 00:39:48.060
Then the fourth state is
DELETED where the image

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:49.019
is flagged as deleted--

00:39:49.019 --> 00:39:50.518
it's not actually
removed from disk.

00:39:50.518 --> 00:39:52.680
You have to do that
separately-- but it's no longer

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:56.110
visible and available.

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:58.480
So you can do
automated obsolescence.

00:39:58.480 --> 00:40:01.800
So what this means is that, when
you tell a version of an image

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:05.520
to be deprecated, there's
two extra flags you can pass.

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:08.680
There's an OBSOLETE in,
and there's a DELETE in.

00:40:08.680 --> 00:40:10.720
And this can be a
relative amount of time--

00:40:10.720 --> 00:40:11.850
so in seven days--

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:13.950
or an absolute amount
of time-- a particular

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:15.480
date that you give it.

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:18.480
So as part of your processes--
ideally an automated baking

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:19.346
process--

00:40:19.346 --> 00:40:21.720
you can say, right, here's
the latest version of an image

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:24.750
I just published for you
and the previous version

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:25.739
deprecated today.

00:40:25.739 --> 00:40:27.780
So all of my users will
get warnings telling them

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:29.364
this isn't the latest version.

00:40:29.364 --> 00:40:30.780
And automatically,
we'll then make

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:32.730
it obsolete in seven days time.

00:40:32.730 --> 00:40:34.169
We'll delete it in 14 days time.

00:40:34.169 --> 00:40:36.210
It's another thing you
don't have to think about.

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:38.420
It will just run through
that lifecycle for you,

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:40.105
and users should treat
it appropriately.

00:40:40.105 --> 00:40:42.480
If people start complaining
that the version of the image

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:45.300
isn't available, they need to
change their deployment scripts

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:49.581
to use the image family rather
than particular versions.

00:40:49.581 --> 00:40:51.580
So we talked about three
different things there.

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.200
We talked about control.

00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.770
Identity is super important
in any cloud provider,

00:40:56.770 --> 00:40:59.170
particularly Google
Cloud, because all

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:02.720
of your access to resources is
focused around your identity.

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:05.200
There's much less of a
focus on network controls,

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:07.780
particularly, with more
of the managed services.

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:10.300
Service accounts are really
powerful if you use them

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:10.800
correctly.

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:13.341
And actually, there are lots of
little details around who can

00:41:13.341 --> 00:41:14.740
access that service account.

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:16.948
That's definitely something
I'd recommend you go away

00:41:16.948 --> 00:41:18.880
and review, probably,
with Forseti

00:41:18.880 --> 00:41:21.570
to see who has that control.

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:23.500
Visibility is out there.

00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:26.640
You can see everything that
happens inside Google Cloud,

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:28.590
but not by default.
And you can't

00:41:28.590 --> 00:41:30.840
wait until there's an
incident to go and find that,

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:32.760
because, unless the
logs are on, you

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:34.500
can't go backwards and see it.

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.630
So you need to review
data access logging.

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:38.740
You need to review
VPC firewall logging.

00:41:38.740 --> 00:41:41.310
And if it's appropriate,
turn that on today

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:43.212
to prepare you for the future.

00:41:43.212 --> 00:41:44.920
And we talked a bit
about some solutions.

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:47.610
How do we combine different
GCP services together

00:41:47.610 --> 00:41:50.160
to get you to a point that
you can run your applications

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:52.860
and services more
securely and get

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:55.210
you prepared for a Beyondcorp
world of the future

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:58.290
where all your employees can
access remotely, securely,

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:01.230
and do whatever they need to.

00:42:01.230 --> 00:42:02.980
Thank you very much
for attending my talk.

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:04.604
I'll be able to take
questions outside.

00:42:04.604 --> 00:42:06.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:09.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

